Client-chosen IOSurface row pitches rounded up to 256 (spessurfround, default 1).

all47 (1115 MHz measured 1114, rwcx 416 / rwmx 128 acked, no faults) took 35-75 ms of GPU time a
frame, no better than 785 MHz: the GPU waits on memory. Its census: ~8 Mpx of clears and ~19 Mpx of
copies a frame. 0166 sized the surfaces the kernel and Metal choose, but SpringBoard (no Metal
device) still asked for 787x1705 at 3152/3200 and 1125x2436 at 4512 bytes a row, which metal2vulkan
shadows. IOSurface may give a larger pitch than asked and clients read it back, so single-plane,
kernel-allocated surfaces now get their rows rounded up to SWEET_SURFACE_ROW_ALIGN and the allocation
grows to match.

--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
@@ -485,6 +485,24 @@
 	    sweet_row_bytes(s->width, s->elementWidth, s->bytesPerElement));
 	if (s->bytesPerRow < (uint64_t)sweet_div_up(s->width, s->elementWidth) * s->bytesPerElement) {
 		return kIOReturnBadArgument;
+	}
+	/*
+	 * A client's own IOSurfaceBytesPerRow rounded up to SWEET_SURFACE_ROW_ALIGN (spessurfround,
+	 * default 1). all47: SpringBoard (no Metal device, so not sized by 0166's answers) still made
+	 * 787x1705 at 3152/3200 and 1125x2436 at 4512, which metal2vulkan shadows (~19 Mpx of copies a
+	 * frame). IOSurface may pick a larger pitch than asked ("kIOSurfaceBytesPerRow ... may be
+	 * larger"); clients read it back (IOSurfaceGetBytesPerRow), the allocation grows to match.
+	 * Not for client-owned memory or planar surfaces.
+	 */
+	static int round_rows = -1;
+	if (round_rows < 0) {
+		uint32_t v = 1;
+		(void)PE_parse_boot_argn("spessurfround", &v, sizeof(v));
+		round_rows = v != 0;
+	}
+	if (round_rows && props->getObject("IOSurfaceAddress") == NULL &&
+	    props->getObject("IOSurfacePlaneInfo") == NULL && (s->bytesPerRow % SWEET_SURFACE_ROW_ALIGN) != 0) {
+		s->bytesPerRow = (s->bytesPerRow + SWEET_SURFACE_ROW_ALIGN - 1) & ~(uint32_t)(SWEET_SURFACE_ROW_ALIGN - 1);
 	}
 	end = (uint64_t)s->offset + (uint64_t)s->bytesPerRow * sweet_div_up(s->height, s->elementHeight);
 
